# text = And the things which are upon these plates pleasing me, because of the prophecies of the coming of Christ; and my fathers knowing that many of them have been fulfilled; yea, and I also know that as many things as have been prophesied concerning us down to this day have been fulfilled, and as many as go beyond this day must surely come to pass--
# sent_id = 4
# llm_note = This is an anacoluthon/complex sentence. "The things ... pleasing me" is an absolute participial construction (nominative absolute), not a finite clause; "pleasing" (9) is the participial head with "things" (3) as nsubj. "my fathers knowing" (23-25) is a second absolute participial conjoined to the first. The main finite clause "I also know" (37-39) is introduced by "and" (36) after "yea" (34). The double "as many ... as" (41-44 and 59-61) are comparative correlatives within the ccomp of "know". The sentence trails off with "--" (71).
